3. System Architecture

The system comprises five logical layers, each with specific hardening against the vulnerabilities identified in earlier drafts.

3.1 Layer 1 — Wearable Sensing and On‑Device Inference

The wearable device—available in belt‑clip, pendant (magnetic lanyard), badge‑clip, and smartwatch form factors—contains the following sensor suite:

· Photoplethysmography (PPG) heart‑rate sensor (Maxim MAX86178)
· 6‑axis inertial measurement unit (BMI270): 3‑axis accelerometer, 3‑axis gyroscope
· Digital MEMS microphone (Knowles SPH0655)
· GNSS receiver (u‑blox M10) with GPS, GLONASS, Galileo
· Barometric pressure sensor
· Magnetometer (used for orientation and mounting‑mode detection)
· Ambient light sensor

All sensor streams are processed inside a Trusted Execution Environment (ARM TrustZone‑M) on a system‑on‑chip that integrates a quad‑core Cortex‑A55 application processor and a neural processing unit delivering at least 1 TOPS for inference. Raw biometric and audio data never leave the TEE unless an emergency alert is confirmed and a specific data package is released under encryption.

Key hardening measures:

· Adaptive baseline learning: During a 2‑week calibration period (and continuously thereafter), the device learns the user’s personal motion signature, resting heart rate, routine paths, and typical activity variance. Detection thresholds are adjusted per user, preventing a 25‑year‑old jogger from generating the same false‑alert rate as a 75‑year‑old with limited mobility.
· Mounting‑mode detection: The device automatically detects whether it is worn on the belt, pendant, badge, or wrist, and switches to the corresponding fall‑detection algorithm. Wrist‑mode uses gyroscope‑augmented algorithms to mitigate higher false‑positive rates from natural arm movement, while torso‑mounted modes leverage the magnetometer and accelerometer for the highest accuracy.
· Stealth mode: For domestic violence or hostage scenarios, the user can activate a covert alert via a pre‑defined gesture (triple tap on the device case) or a voice passphrase. In stealth mode, all LEDs and haptic feedback are disabled; the device silently transmits an encrypted alert to a dedicated survivor‑support monitoring queue. The operator is trained to listen without speaking and to dispatch help without alerting the perpetrator.

3.2 Layer 2 — Edge Communication Gateway

The device carries a multi‑radio subsystem that automatically falls back through connectivity options:

1. 5G NR Sub‑6 (primary, lowest latency)
2. Wi‑Fi 6E (indoor and campus environments)
3. LEO satellite (Iridium‑based NTN) for remote areas and infrastructure failure
4. 3GPP Release 18 sidelink for device‑to‑device mesh relay (effective only at sufficient device density)

An encrypted messaging bus (MQTT‑S over DTLS 1.3) handles alert package transmission. Power‑aware degradation is implemented: when the battery falls below 20%, the device suspends continuous audio streaming and switches to periodic location beacons (every 10 minutes) via the lowest‑power available radio, extending survivability to over 24 hours. The full radio power budget is detailed in Section 6.

3.3 Layer 3 — Guardian Cloud and Monitoring Centres

The cloud layer ingests encrypted alert packages, verifies their digital signatures, and routes them to the appropriate monitoring centre. The architecture mirrors the operational models of existing 24/7 professional monitoring services (Bay Alarm Medical, Lively, SHEQSY/ARC) with the following Guardian‑specific enhancements:

· Tier‑1 (Medical/Fall): Automatic alert to pre‑designated contacts; optional direct dispatch to emergency medical services if the user does not cancel within 30 seconds. This tier requires no human operator verification.
· Tier‑2 (Duress Inference): When the composite threat score indicates potential distress but with lower confidence, an encrypted event package (sensor scores, location, optional audio snippet) is sent to a live operator. The operator must review and attempt two‑way communication within 15 seconds; if no verification is achieved, the event escalates to Tier 3.
· Tier‑3 (Incapacitation Confirmed): Fired when the composite score crosses a high‑confidence threshold and the user fails to respond to haptic/audio prompts for 60 seconds. The system performs fully automatic dispatch to 911/112, drones (if available), and emergency contacts with pre‑verified location.

All operator interactions are logged immutably and made available to the user in an audit log.

Legal‑hold and privacy infrastructure:

· Upon receipt of a lawful warrant or subpoena, the data disclosed is the encrypted alert package only. The decryption key is held by the user (or their designated legal representative) and is not escrowed by Guardian AI. The user is notified of the request unless legally prohibited.
· An auto‑delete policy removes all non‑preserved event recordings (audio/video) 72 hours after the event. Users may explicitly mark recordings for retention as evidence.
· The company publishes an annual transparency report detailing the number of legal requests, operator interventions, and aggregated false‑alarm statistics.

3.4 Layer 4 — Smart City and Drone Integration (Conditional Add‑On)

Integration with municipal drone first‑responder programmes and smart infrastructure is treated as a premium, conditional add‑on. It is not required for the core Guardian AI service. A municipality qualifies for drone integration only when it meets all of the following prerequisites:

· FAA Part 107 waiver or BVLOS (Beyond Visual Line of Sight) certification for drone operations.
· NG911 or RapidSOS‑compatible computer‑aided dispatch (CAD) with live integration tested over a 30‑day dry‑run.
· Signed liability and medical oversight agreement covering AED and naloxone delivery by drone.
· Dock infrastructure capable of pre‑armed launch in ≤5 seconds (consistent with BRINC Guardian drone benchmarks).

When these conditions are met, Guardian AI can trigger drone launch, traffic signal preemption (C‑V2X SPaT/MAP messages), and public address system activation via standard APIs.

3.5 Layer 5 — End‑User Application

A companion mobile and web application provides:

· Device configuration, including mounting mode, contact lists, and stealth‑mode setup.
· Real‑time audit log of all events and operator interactions.
· Consent dashboard: user controls which sensors are active, which data can be transmitted, and under what circumstances.
· Evidence vault for preserved recordings, with user‑managed encryption keys.

The app never receives raw biometric or continuous audio data; it displays only encrypted metadata and system status.

