4. Mathematical Foundations

4.1 Sensor Signal Models

Each sensor channel is treated as a discrete‑time stochastic process. At time  t , the observation vector is:

\mathbf{x}_t = [\ \text{HR}_t,\ \text{HRV}_t,\ \mathbf{a}_t,\ \boldsymbol{\omega}_t,\ \mathbf{m}_t,\ \ell_t\ ]

Where:

·  \text{HR}_t  is heart rate in beats per minute (BPM).
·  \text{HRV}_t  is the root mean square of successive differences (RMSSD) of inter‑beat intervals, in milliseconds.
·  \mathbf{a}_t \in \mathbb{R}^3  is tri‑axial acceleration in units of g.
·  \boldsymbol{\omega}_t \in \mathbb{R}^3  is tri‑axial angular velocity in rad/s.
·  \mathbf{m}_t  is a 13‑dimensional Mel‑frequency cepstral coefficient (MFCC) vector extracted from a 25 ms audio frame, updated every 10 ms.
·  \ell_t  encodes latitude, longitude, altitude, indoor/outdoor flag, and land‑use category from offline map tiles.

Noise characteristics are calibrated per device revision:

· PPG: motion‑artifact‑filtered variance approximately 2 BPM² when stationary; adaptive filtering using the accelerometer signal reduces motion noise.
· Accelerometer: noise density 150 µg/√Hz, bias stability 0.1 mg.
· Audio: signal‑to‑noise ratio ≥ 65 dB for voice‑band signals.

4.2 On‑Device Machine Learning Models

All inference runs on the device NPU. Models are quantised to int8 and compiled for deterministic latency ≤ 50 ms end‑to‑end per sensor window.

Fall Detection: A Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN) with 3 layers, dilation rates [1,2,4], kernel size 5, operates on a 3‑second sliding window of \mathbf{a}_t, \boldsymbol{\omega}_t. It outputs a probability p_f. The model is trained on the URFD and Sisfall public datasets, achieving 99.48% accuracy in benchmark tests. A fall is flagged when p_f > 0.9.

Cardiac Anomaly Detection: A lightweight transformer encoder (4 attention heads, 2 layers) processes 10‑second windows of HR and HRV. It outputs an abnormality score s_c \in [0,1] optimised for sensitivity to asystole, severe bradycardia (HR < 40 BPM), and extreme tachycardia (HR > 180 BPM). The model is trained on PhysioNet/CinC Challenge datasets.

Audio Event Classifier: A YamNet‑lite depthwise‑separable CNN converts 0.96 s audio segments into an embedding, followed by a threat‑specific logistic regression that computes an audio distress score s_a. The classifier is trained on general environmental sounds and distress vocalisations (screams, crying, aggressive voice) but is not optimised for fine‑grained assault‑specific sounds. It is used exclusively as part of duress inference, not for autonomous assault declaration.

Location Context Score: A Bayesian belief network computes p_\ell, the probability that the current location and time are anomalous relative to the user’s routine. Inputs include hour of day, land‑use category, and distance from a Gaussian Mixture Model of frequently visited locations learned during the calibration phase.

4.3 Multi‑Modal Fusion and Duress Inference

The system does not claim to detect assault. Instead it computes a Duress Score (DS) designed solely to flag ambiguous distress for human operator verification. The DS is defined as:

\text{DS} = \sigma\!\left( \beta_0 + \beta_1 \cdot s_f + \beta_2 \cdot s_c + \beta_3 \cdot s_a + \beta_4 \cdot p_\ell + \beta_5 \cdot \text{immobility\_duration} \right)

where \sigma is the logistic sigmoid, and \beta_i are weights trained via L2‑regularised logistic regression on a composite dataset of true emergencies and nuisance events. The immobility duration (in seconds, capped at 120) accounts for the increased likelihood of a genuine emergency the longer a person remains motionless after a possible fall or impact.

For Tier‑1 (medical/fall) events, the system uses a separate Medical Emergency Score that places higher weight on fall probability and cardiac abnormality, with lower influence from audio and location.

Adaptive thresholding: Each user’s thresholds \tau_{\text{user}} are derived from a global base threshold \tau_{\text{global}} plus a user‑specific offset:

\tau_{\text{user}} = \tau_{\text{global}} + \Delta(\text{activity\_profile}, \text{age}, \text{resting\_HR\_variance})

The offset \Delta is computed from a look‑up table calibrated on population data, ensuring that highly active young users have higher thresholds for Tier‑2 alerts, reducing false alarms. The global thresholds are set to achieve the false‑positive‑rate targets described in Section 5.

4.4 Privacy and Security Protocols

On‑device processing: All feature extraction and model inference are performed inside the TEE. Raw sensor data is encrypted with a device‑unique symmetric key before storage in external flash, which can only be read by the TEE.

Selective transmission: Only when an emergency is confirmed does the device assemble an alert package containing:

· Event type and duress/medical score.
· Timestamp and GPS coordinates.
· Optionally, 60 seconds of pre‑trigger audio (AAC 32 kbps) and 30 seconds of post‑trigger video (H.265, 720p) if equipped with a camera.
· A digital signature using the device’s hardware‑backed key (HMAC‑SHA256).

Chain of custody: The alert payload is encrypted with AES‑256‑GCM using a session key, which is wrapped with the public key of the recipient monitoring centre. No cloud intermediary can decrypt the content. Evidence integrity is preserved by building a Merkle tree over the sensor timeline, with each block signed by an incremental hash.

Differential privacy for model improvement: Users who opt in to federated learning contribute gradient updates perturbed with Gaussian noise, achieving (\epsilon, \delta)‑differential privacy with \epsilon = 1.0 per user per month.

