8. Deployment Pathway and Regulatory Architecture

The system is released in phases, each with specific technical milestones and regulatory hurdles.

Phase 0 — Duress‑Inference Dataset and Field Study (0–18 months)

· IRB‑approved study in partnership with trauma centres and law enforcement to collect labelled acoustic and kinematic data of distress and assault events.
· No commercial product; algorithm development only.

Phase 1 — Medical/Fall Detection for Elderly and Chronically Ill (12–18 months, concurrent with Phase 0)

· Device sold as a medical alert wearable. Tier‑1 medical/fall detection only; no duress inference or audio classification active.
· Regulatory: FDA Class II 510(k) clearance or exemption; Medicare/insurance reimbursement alignment.
· Liability: medical device liability insurance with an A‑rated carrier.

Phase 2 — Lone Worker Safety (18–36 months)

· Add Tier‑2 duress inference with operator verification and satellite fallback. Stealth mode introduced. Enterprise dashboard with compliance reporting.
· Regulatory: OSHA‑compliant safety equipment; employer indemnification contracts.
· Liability: E&O insurance policy; enterprise client assumes proper‑use liability.

Phase 3 — General Consumer Launch (36–60 months)

· Full duress inference and medical detection available to consumers. Product marketed explicitly as a “duress‑alert device with fall detection,” not as an assault detector. All marketing materials carry clear caveats.
· Regulatory: FCC/CE certification; state‑by‑state recording‑law compliance; UL 2900‑2‑1 penetration test; independent bias audit of detection rates by race, gender, and age.
· Liability: consumer electronics Terms of Service limit liability; optional personal injury legal fund subscription add‑on.

Phase 4 — Municipal Drone Integration (Conditional, 60+ months)

· Activated only when a municipality meets the prerequisites listed in Section 3.4.
· Inter‑governmental agreement with mutual hold‑harmless for drone incidents.

