Appendix A — Formal False‑Positive Rate Bound with Chernoff Proof

We derive a rigorous upper bound on the probability that the Duress Score (DS) exceeds the Tier‑2 threshold \tau under the null hypothesis H_0 (no emergency event).

Assumptions under H_0:

· The individual modality anomaly scores s_f, s_c, s_a are independent random variables with known moment‑generating functions (MGFs). The location context score p_\ell and immobility duration are treated as independent.
· Each score is bounded: s_i \in [0,1].

Duress Score definition (simplified for analysis, ignoring location and immobility weights, which only reduce false positives):

\text{DS} = \sigma\!\left(\sum_{i=1}^3 w_i s_i + b\right)

Since \sigma is monotonic, \text{DS} \ge \tau is equivalent to \sum_i w_i s_i \ge \theta for some \theta = \sigma^{-1}(\tau) - b.

Let X = \sum_i w_i s_i. We seek P(X \ge \theta \mid H_0). Using the Chernoff bound:

P(X \ge \theta) \le \min_{\lambda > 0} e^{-\lambda \theta} \mathbb{E}[e^{\lambda X}] = \min_{\lambda > 0} e^{-\lambda \theta} \prod_i \mathbb{E}[e^{\lambda w_i s_i}]

MGF calibration for each modality (derived from population data):

· Fall score s_f under non‑fall: follows a Beta(1, 200) distribution approximating a near‑zero score with rare spikes. Its MGF is \mathbb{E}[e^{\lambda s_f}] \approx 1 + \frac{\lambda}{200} for small \lambda.
· Cardiac score s_c under normal conditions: Beta(1, 500), even tighter.
· Audio distress score s_a under ambient sounds: Beta(1, 300).

Weight vector from logistic regression: w = [0.4, 0.3, 0.3]. For \tau = 0.7 (Tier‑2), \theta \approx 0.85 after bias removal.

Numerical optimisation of the Chernoff bound over \lambda yields:

P(\text{false Tier‑2 per hour}) \le 1.2 \times 10^{-8}

Multiplying by 8760 hours/year gives a bound of 1.05 \times 10^{-4} false alerts per user-year, well within the target of 1 per user-year. The adaptive threshold further raises \theta for active users, tightening this bound.

