1. Idle (no emergency): 15 mW → life = 1.71 Wh / 0.015 W = 114 hours (4.75 days).
2. Tier‑1 burst: GPS + short data (10 seconds). Energy per event: (15+30+600) mW × 10s = 0.0018 Wh. Negligible.
3. Tier‑2 continuous streaming (5G): 15 + 30 (GPS 1Hz) + 600 (5G) + 2 (mic) + 50 (audio processing) = 697 mW. Battery life: 1.71 / 0.697 ≈ 2.45 hours. We round down to 2.1 hours to account for radio power amplifier inefficiencies.
4. Tier‑2 via satellite: Replace 5G with satellite average 200 mW plus burst overhead, total ≈ 15+30+200+2+50+ (occasional 1000 mW burst) = effective 310 mW average, but with higher burst, total around 1.2 W peak for short intervals; average 400 mW, giving ~4.3 hours. However, satellite SBD transmission is only possible in short windows, so audio streaming may be limited; life 1.4 hours if streaming continuously, but we implement low‑duty‑cycle mode.
5. Tier‑3 power‑degraded (<20% battery): Only location beacons every 10 min. Each beacon: GNSS fix (30 s) and satellite SBD (1 s burst). Energy per beacon: (15+30)×30 + 1000×1 = 4500 mW·s = 1.25 mWh. With 10 min interval, average power = 1.25 mWh / 600 s = 0.00208 W = 2.08 mW. Plus base sensing 15 mW, total ≈ 17.1 mW. Remaining energy at 20% battery: 0.342 Wh. Life = 0.342 / 0.0171 = 20 hours. Additional solar trickle (50 mW) during daylight extends this to over 30 hours.

Graph description (textual): Plot of battery percentage vs. time for three scenarios: idle, Tier‑2 5G streaming, Tier‑3 degraded with solar. Idle shows linear decay over 4.7 days. Streaming drops from 100% to 0% in 2.1 hours. Degraded mode maintains above 10% for over 24 hours.

