Appendix D — STRIDE Threat Tree with Detailed Attack Paths

We expand the STRIDE categories with specific attack scenarios.

Spoofing

· False sensor injection: Attacker vibrates device or plays loud screams to trigger false alerts. Mitigation: sensor cross‑correlation (accelerometer vs. microphone coherence) to detect artificial patterns; rate‑limit 3 alerts per hour.
· GPS spoofing: Overpowers GNSS signal to simulate remote location. Mitigation: IMU dead reckoning detects sudden location jumps; cell tower/Wi‑Fi location cross‑check.

Tampering

· Physical opening: Attacker removes enclosure to access JTAG. Mitigation: tamper‑evident mesh in enclosure; secure element erases keys on tamper detect; device locks.
· Firmware replacement: Malicious firmware loaded via debug port. Mitigation: secure boot with hardware root of trust; firmware must be signed; binary transparency log prevents silent rollback.

Repudiation

· User denies alert: Device logs show hardware‑signed alert timestamp and sensor data; cryptographic proof non‑repudiable.

Information Disclosure

· Eavesdropping on radio: Passive interception of DTLS traffic. Mitigation: perfect forward secrecy (ECDHE); session keys ephemeral.
· Cloud database breach: Encrypted payloads stored; keys held by users; breach yields only ciphertext.

Denial of Service

· Radio jamming: Broadband jamming of 5G/Wi‑Fi. Mitigation: satellite L‑band narrowband resistant; mesh relay on different frequency; store‑and‑forward when connectivity resumes.
· Battery drain attack: Repeated alert triggers to exhaust battery. Mitigation: rate‑limit and forced cool‑down.

Elevation of Privilege

· Insider operator misuse: Operator views data without cause. Mitigation: role‑based access, audit trails, and data minimisation (only encrypted payload visible without user key).
· Legal coercion: Government demands backdoor. Mitigation: no key escrow; transparency reporting.

Intimate Partner Abuse

· Abuser uses device to track victim: Location sharing with contacts can be weaponised. Mitigation: user can disable location sharing with all contacts and switch to first‑responder‑only mode; stealth mode hides alert from abuser.

